welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn wokes your host and today our  guest is Professor Francesca travail  Otto a professor of history at Yale  professor trovato specializes in the  social and economic history of Italy  continental Europe and the Mediterranean  in the early modern period she's the  author of a book on Venetian glass  manufacturing and she has also published  several essays on craft guilds women's  work and merchant networks we were going  to talk with her today about her newest  book the familiarity of strangers the  Sephardic diaspora Livorno and  cross-cultural trade in the early modern  period  welcome professor trovato very nice to  meet here maryland's okay in your newest  book which will be out in May you look  at you take a new approach to  cross-cultural trade  tell us a little bit about the premise  in a long history of European and  Western thought and to some extent in  larger popular imagination Commerce and  toleration often go hand-in-hand and  historically it's true that society that  have engaged in trade with groups of  different religious and ethnic  background especially port cities or  coastal society tend to be more upper  mobile more open-minded if we want but  we also know that historically trade has  coexisted with war conquest with the  segregation of merchants group within  for example with in port cities and with  religious prejudice more more generally  so the premise of of this book is that  in order to trade across religious and  ethnic boundaries merchants need to have  some understanding of each other's  culture at the very least they  to have a language in common sometimes  they hire translators but they need to  be able to communicate they need to have  a sense of what legal norms can be used  in order to adjudicate potential  disputes and how to find recourse in  case of fraud but merchants need not to  embrace each other culture you know  fully so that you can trade with  somebody if you have enough trust that  say they will repay your credit they  will fulfill your orders they will abide  by the promises that make to you but it  doesn't mean if you're a merger that you  want to marry your daughter to them or  that you want to invite them to the same  place of worship where you go quickly  for example okay and what drew you to  this topic I'm fascinated by how market  relations work in different times and in  different places and the history of  Europe between around 1500 and 1800  which is the period that historians  called the early modern period it's  particularly fascinating because this is  a time when European societies became  more open more uh permobil when certain  stigma against merchants they kind of  begun to decline in comparison to the  previous a feudal period it's also a  period when certain religious groups and  ethnic diasporas that were heavily  involved in trade including Jews are  given unprecedented rights relatively to  the time period in part because of the  perceived commercial abilities of these  groups and yet at the same time European  society in this period in the early  modern period remained very hierarchical  and segregation persisted and religious  prejudice are persisted so I was  interested first of all in understanding  how cross-cultural trade could work in  this context and also what it might mean  for the people involved in it and  I say this because we tend to assume  that you know Jews traded with Jews or  Quakers traded with trader excuse me  Quakers traded with Quakers or Hugo  knows traded with humour knows and on  the other hand some historians and some  scholars in the social sciences tend to  assume that for these communities to  trade with each other there need to be a  modern legal system that emerges and  that adjudicate disputes when they arise  but the everyday reality of trade I  found was more complicated and in fact  social and legal forms of enforcement of  commercial obligations tended to coexist  so why live Lorna why did you choose  this city in Italy and give us an idea  of where it's located in Italy as well  Livorno is a coastal port city in  Tuscany and I was drawn to Livorno by  both intellectual and scholarly reason  and to some extent by chance and my  interest in Livorno has to do with the  fact that most European scholarship on  early modern global trade focuses on the  Atlantic and to some extent of the  Indian Ocean and we tend to forget that  the Mediterranean continued to be an  important geopolitical and commercial  area and the one in particular was a  major international hub in the late 16th  and early 17th century the Medici Grand  Dukes of Tuscany  transformed a small village into a large  international port city and Livorno in  fact has an English version of its name  it's called Leghorn because it was the  British outpost in the Mediterranean  there were a number of foreign and  religious and ethnic minorities who  operated there as merchants and in  particular which was a part of my  interest Livorno was a had a very large  community of Iberian Jews Sephardic Jews  the descendants of those Jews who had  been expelled from Spain and for  ninety-two the largest community of  Sephardic Jews in Europe and the new  world was in Amsterdam and it studied  has been studied quite a lot and Livorno  has not been studied very much and when  I say that I was also drawn to laverna  by chance is because one of the reason  why Livorno has not been investigated as  much as others say you know London or  Philadelphia or more well-known port  cities is because it's archival records  a rather poor part were destroyed in the  late 19th century and then during the  bombings of world war ii part went into  flames and i came across by chance i  came across a collection of 13,000 670  business letters that a jewish  partnership in Livorno had written in  the early 18th century to a large  variety of business agents across  mediterranean the Mediterranean Europe  and the Indian Ocean and as I began to  read these letters I found that a  considerable number was addressed to  Christian Catholic particulate merchants  and even to a group of Hindu merchants  in in Goa in Porton which was the  capital of Portuguese India at the time  and I thought that was really  fascinating and I was hooked mm-hmm that  is very interesting so let's talk a  little bit in specifics about their  research you've done I imagine you've  had to travel to lavona which I imagine  was pretty wonderful what else did you  have to do in order to pull the book  together well so these are Jewish  merchants who lived and traded lived in  leborgne and traded from there  were part of an intercontinental  diaspora and also as these letters show  they had extended extensive and and  durable commercial relations with  merchants communities very far  geographically and culturally from them  so I decided to follow the traces you  know to follow the footsteps of these  of these merchants letters and it was  not enough for me to do the research in  Tuscany in order to appreciate the  global dimension of their commercial  activities in Lisbon I found a lot of  interesting material in the archives of  the parish church in which Genoese and  Florentine merchants Catholic merchants  who traded regularly with the Jews in  Livorno congregated in Goa in India I  found some very interesting business  letters written by a group of Hindu  merchants who traded with Europeans  regularly and who were able to do so  partially because they acquire the  etiquette and the standards of business  letter-writing that also had some legal  implications in Paris and in Marseilles  I found a lot of documents that  documented the activities of the Jews of  Livorno in the Mediterranean because in  their commercial activities in the  Ottoman Empire the Jews of Livorno were  under French diplomatic protection in  London I found a lot of last wills  Testaments of Jews of Livorno who  invested part of their money in the  British stock exchange  and public debt in the Netherlands I  found a really interesting lawsuits that  then are related to the bankruptcy of  the partnership I was working on and at  some point a colleague of mine in the  history department at Yale suggested  that I looked at library in Minnesota  where in fact there was a cachet of  letters that was relevant to my project  so it was a lot of fun and really I had  to try to follow a little bit on the on  the under footsteps of these the truly  global partnerships and one of the  interesting things you found in your  research was that business cooperation  could coexist with mistrust Evette with  other social and cultural aspects  explain that phenomenon a little bit in  fact i think that if we appreciate the  technology of trade in this period and  the legal social  cultural organization of European  society in this period we shouldn't be  too surprised by the fact that Economic  Cooperation could go together with  religious mistrust by technology of  trade I mean that long-distance trade  was very risky this was before the  steamship was invented before the  Telegraph existed before International  Tribunal at which you could I you know I  that could adjudicate disputes and  commercial matters came into existence  and that is part of the reason why it  was often the case that merchants will  rely on family members or on  co-religionist in order to conduct their  business across long distances not  because family members or  co-religionists are naturally more you  know competent or more trustworthy but  because it's easier to monitor them and  because they have greater incentives to  fulfill their commercial obligation but  there's more and there is that really  that economic cooperation it's not  synonymous with cultural appreciation so  you could find business letters in which  say a Christian merchant who trades  regularly with individual Jewish  merchants also includes comments that  are derogative and stereotypical about  Jews as a collective and what enduring  your research did you find to be most  surprising I was surprised by the extent  of the connections that existed between  the Mediterranean and the Indian Ocean  in this period the items of trade where  the Mediterranean red coral was exported  to India where it was highly valued and  it was bartered exchanged with Indian  diamonds and other precious stones I was  surprised by the extent to which the  Jews of Livorno were central to this  intercontinental trade and particularly  because by this period very few of them  would travel there in person  and so they did not know did not had met  personally the hinder traders to whom  they entrusted their business and  instead they relied on this a very  highly standardized etiquette of  business right of business letter  writing I was also surprised by the fact  that the business partnership on which I  focused the authors of these voluminous  correspondence went bankrupt  not because they were let down by one of  these distant Hindu agents who they  never met in person but because they at  some point entered into an ill-fated  business venture  with a Persian Jew in the attempt to  sell a 60 carat diamond which is a very  large diamond that which they brought  first to Versailles the French king's  court and later tried to sell on the  auction market for diamonds in London  and Amsterdam and it turned out the  diamond was not of the quality that they  had hoped and expected but also that the  Iranian Jew with whom they had entered  into business was not as reliable as  they had anticipated so I was surprised  that by the kind of tensions within the  broadest world of Jewish business that I  had investigated thank you very much for  being with us here today and sharing  some of your book with us the  familiarity of strangers for more  information about Professor trovato and  true research please visit our website  at yale.edu backslash Macmillan report  be sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Centre for  International and area studies at Yale  you
 

